Tuned atomic integration grids (tv-13.7-N.txt)
===============================================

These files are copied verbatim from HORTON 2.1.0 (data/grids/), where they
were created by Toon Verstraelen in July 2013. Each file is one accuracy
tier: the grids were tuned on diatomic benchmarks (Ahlrichs QZVP basis) to
give on average N digits of precision (N = 3..7) for a variety of molecular
integrals using Becke's partitioning scheme with k=3.

Format: for each element, a line "Z Zeff" (Zeff = effective core charge, the
number of electrons treated explicitly; Zeff < Z means an ECP grid), a line
"PowerRTransform rmin rmax npt" (bohr) and a line with the Lebedev order for
each of the npt radial shells. Radial points: r_i = rmin * (i+1)^p,
i = 0..npt-1, p = ln(rmax/rmin)/ln(npt); radial weights use the composite
Simpson rule in i.

HORTON is distributed under the GNU General Public License version 3 (or
later), the same license as critic2. See http://theochem.github.io/horton/ .

Citation
--------
There is no journal article describing these tables. When using them, cite:
- A. D. Becke, J. Chem. Phys. 88, 2547 (1988), doi:10.1063/1.454033
  (multicenter partitioning scheme the tables were tuned for).
- V. I. Lebedev and D. N. Laikov, Dokl. Math. 59, 477 (1999)
  (angular quadratures).
- HORTON 2.1.0 as software: T. Verstraelen, P. Tecmer, F. Heidar-Zadeh,
  C. E. Gonzalez-Espinoza, M. Chan, T. D. Kim, K. Boguslawski, S. Fias,
  S. Vandenbrande, D. Berrocal, and P. W. Ayers, http://theochem.github.com/horton/
  (2017).
The successor library, which ships the same grid family, is described in
A. Tehrani, X. D. Yang, M. Martinez-Gonzalez, et al., J. Chem. Phys. 160,
172503 (2024), doi:10.1063/5.0202240.
